<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE reference PUBLIC "-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN" "reference.dtd">
<reference id="subjectscheme_mapping">
    <title>Subject scheme class mapping</title>
    <refbody>
        <section>
            <p>The <codeph>subjectscheme</codeph> metadata handler maps instances to iiRDS
                relations based on the following fixed set of top-level iiRDS classes, which are
                built into the plugin. Any class nested underneath one of these top-level classes
                in a subject scheme map, at any depth, is resolved to the same relation. See <xref
                    keyref="subjectscheme_metadata"/> for details about this resolution.</p>
            <table frame="all" rowsep="1" colsep="1" id="table_subjectscheme_mapping">
                <title>Overview of top-level iiRDS classes and iiRDS relations</title>
                <tgroup cols="3">
                    <colspec colname="c1" colnum="1" colwidth="1*"/>
                    <colspec colname="c2" colnum="2" colwidth="1.3*"/>
                    <colspec colname="c3" colnum="3" colwidth="1.3*"/>
                    <thead>
                        <row>
                            <entry>Top-level iiRDS class</entry>
                            <entry>iiRDS relation</entry>
                            <entry>Comment</entry>
                        </row>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <row>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:ProductVariant</codeph></entry>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:relates-to-product-variant</codeph></entry>
                            <entry/>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:ProductFeature</codeph></entry>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:relates-to-product-feature</codeph></entry>
                            <entry/>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:Component</codeph></entry>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:relates-to-component</codeph></entry>
                            <entry/>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:Supply</codeph></entry>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:relates-to-supply</codeph></entry>
                            <entry/>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:Event</codeph></entry>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:relates-to-event</codeph></entry>
                            <entry/>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:Qualification</codeph></entry>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:relates-to-qualification</codeph></entry>
                            <entry>Includes the predefined classes <codeph>iirds:Role</codeph> and
                                    <codeph>iirds:SkillLevel</codeph>.</entry>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:InformationSubject</codeph></entry>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:has-subject</codeph></entry>
                            <entry/>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:ProductLifeCyclePhase</codeph></entry>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:relates-to-product-lifecycle-phase</codeph></entry>
                            <entry>Example instance: <codeph>iirds:Repair</codeph>.</entry>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:DocumentType</codeph></entry>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:has-document-type</codeph> (on
                                    <codeph>iirds:Document</codeph>) or <codeph
                                    >iirds:is-applicable-for-document-type</codeph> (on
                                    <codeph>iirds:Topic</codeph>)</entry>
                            <entry>The relation depends on whether the metadata is assigned to the
                                root map (<codeph>iirds:Document</codeph>) or a topic. Example
                                instance: <codeph>iirds:OperatingInstructions</codeph>.</entry>
                        </row>
                        <row>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:DocumentCategory</codeph> (iiRDS handover
                                domain)</entry>
                            <entry><codeph>iirds:has-document-category</codeph></entry>
                            <entry>Only applied when the metadata is assigned to the root map
                                    (<codeph>iirds:Document</codeph>).</entry>
                        </row>
                    </tbody>
                </tgroup>
            </table>
            <p>A class that is not one of these top-level classes, and not nested underneath one
                of them, is not mapped to an iiRDS relation by the <codeph>subjectscheme</codeph>
                handler. Adding a genuinely new top-level class, one that is not a descendant of
                any class in this table, requires a change to the plugin. See <xref
                    keyref="metadata_handler_behavior"/> for this and other current
                limitations.</p>
        </section>
        <section>
            <title>Predefined iiRDS subclasses and instances</title>
            <p>The iiRDS standard already defines many classes and instances underneath the
                top-level classes above, for common cases such as document types, product life cycle
                phases, and information subjects. For easy application of these predefined entries,
                this plugin ships with a subject scheme map that was generated from the iiRDS RDF
                schemas. After integrating this subject scheme map into your DITA content, the
                corresponding <codeph>&lt;subjectdef></codeph> entries can be referenced using
                    <codeph>&lt;data keyref="..."></codeph>. Custom subclasses and instances are
                typically needed for project- or domain-specific concepts, such as product variants
                and components, that are not already part of the iiRDS standard. These can be
                integrated into the subject scheme map using custom IRIs and then referenced the
                same way as the predefined entries.</p>
            <p>See <xref keyref="subjectscheme_authoring"/> for best practices on extending and
                using the subject scheme map.</p>
        </section>
    </refbody>
    <related-links>
        <link keyref="subjectscheme_authoring"/>
        <link keyref="subjectscheme_generator"/>
    </related-links>
</reference>
